 "flow": {
  "start": "opening",
  "nodes": {
   "opening": {
    "type": "converse",
    "task": "You asked whether you are speaking with {{title}} {{last_name}}. Beyond saying that you are Freya Bank's digital assistant and that the call is being taped, say nothing: no reason for the call, no product, amount, debt, payment or collections.\n- If the person asks a question (who is this, are you a robot, is this a scam, what is this about), answer it in one sentence without giving the reason for the call, then ask again: \"Am I speaking with {{title}} {{last_name}}?\"\n- Only \"yes\", \"that's me\", \"speaking\" or the customer's own name confirms. \"Hello\", \"go ahead\" and \"who is this\" do not.\n- If it is still unclear after your second ask, call transition_to_end_unclear.\n- If the person says twice that they do not want to talk, call transition_to_end_declined_pre.\n- If they ask for a human: ask once whether you can help; if they insist, call transfer_to_agent with queue general.\nNo other tool call in this step.",
    "enter": {
     "tts": "Hello, I am a digital assistant calling from Freya Bank. Please note that we are recording this call. Am I speaking with {{title}} {{last_name}}?"
    },
    "extract": [
     {
      "name": "who",
      "type": "string",
      "enum_values": [
       "confirmed",
       "someone_else",
       "wrong_number",
       "deceased",
       "unclear"
      ],
      "description": "Who answered, from the person's replies to 'Am I speaking with <the customer>?'. confirmed: says yes, that's me, speaking, or says the customer's own name. someone_else: says they are a different person (a relative, partner, friend, colleague) or that the customer is not there or not available. wrong_number: says this number does not belong to the customer or they do not know the customer. deceased: says the customer has died. unclear: anything else, such as hello, go ahead, who is this, or a question.",
      "min_confidence": 0.5
     }
    ],
    "tools": [
     "transfer_to_agent"
    ],
    "tool_overrides": {
     "transfer_to_agent": {
      "description": "Hand the call to a person. CALL it only when the customer insists on a human after you asked once whether you can help. Queue general.",
      "fields": {
       "queue": {
        "enum": [
         "general"
        ]
       }
      }
     }
    },
    "turns": 8,
    "overflow": "end:unclear",
    "routes": [
     {
      "to": "setup",
      "when": {
       "eq": [
        "who",
        "confirmed"
       ]
      },
      "description": "The person confirmed they are the customer"
     },
     {
      "to": "setup",
      "when": {
       "eq": [
        "who",
        "someone_else"
       ]
      },
      "description": "Someone else answered"
     },
     {
      "to": "end:wrong_number",
      "when": {
       "eq": [
        "who",
        "wrong_number"
       ]
      },
      "description": "Wrong number"
     },
     {
      "to": "end:deceased",
      "when": {
       "eq": [
        "who",
        "deceased"
       ]
      },
      "description": "The customer has died"
     },
     {
      "to": "end:transfer",
      "when": {
       "set": "transfer_queue"
      },
      "description": "The call was transferred"
     },
     {
      "to": "end:unclear",
      "tool_call": true,
      "description": "After the second ask it is still unclear whether the customer is the person on the line."
     },
     {
      "to": "end:declined_pre",
      "tool_call": true,
      "description": "The person said a second time that they do not want to talk."
     }
    ]
   },
   "setup": {
    "type": "clock",
    "next": "identity",
    "routes": [
     {
      "to": "third_party",
      "when": {
       "eq": [
        "who",
        "someone_else"
       ]
      },
      "description": "Someone else answered"
     }
    ]
   },
   "third_party": {
    "type": "converse",
    "task": "You are speaking with someone who is NOT {{title}} {{last_name}}. Say nothing about why you are calling: no product, amount, debt, payment, collections or account, even if they ask or offer to pay. Never ask them security questions. Your only job is a time to call {{title}} {{last_name}} back.\n{{today_facts}}\n{{callback_facts}}\n- When the person names a day and a time inside that window, call schedule_callback with the date (YYYY-MM-DD) and the time (HH:MM, 24 hour). If they give only a day or only a part of the day, ask for the time.\n- If the day or time is outside the window, say when you can call and ask ONCE for another time. If that is also outside the window, call transition_to_end_no_callback without booking.\n- If they cannot or will not give a time, call transition_to_end_no_callback.\n- If they say the number does not belong to the customer, call transition_to_end_wrong_number.\n- If they say the customer has died, call transition_to_end_deceased at once, without speaking (the closing line says \"I am sorry for your loss.\"). Ask nothing else.",
    "enter": {
     "llm": "Someone other than the customer answered. If they asked what the call is about, say only that you need to speak with {{title}} {{last_name}} personally. Then ask when {{title}} {{last_name}} can be reached."
    },
    "tools": [
     "schedule_callback"
    ],
    "turns": 12,
    "overflow": "end:no_callback",
    "routes": [
     {
      "to": "end:callback",
      "when": {
       "eq": [
        "callback_state",
        "booked"
       ]
      },
      "description": "Callback booked"
     },
     {
      "to": "end:no_callback",
      "tool_call": true,
      "description": "No callback inside the window could be agreed: the second proposal was also outside it, or the person gave no time."
     },
     {
      "to": "end:wrong_number",
      "tool_call": true,
      "description": "The person says the number does not belong to the customer."
     },
     {
      "to": "end:deceased",
      "tool_call": true,
      "description": "The person says the customer has died."
     }
    ]
   },
   "identity": {
    "type": "verify",
    "task": "You are checking the customer's identity. Until it passes, do not say why you are calling and do not mention collections, debt, payment, any product or amount.\nAsk the security questions one at a time, in this order: 1) the date of birth, 2) the last four digits of the phone number on file.\n- Every time the customer gives an answer, call verify_identity with that field and answer. date_of_birth: convert what they said to YYYY-MM-DD; a date said only in numbers is day, then month, then year. phone_last4: the four digits. Customers often give an answer in pieces over several messages. Join the pieces of consecutive messages in order. If what you have so far is incomplete, ask only for the missing part - do not ask the whole question again. As soon as a message contains a complete answer (a day, a month and a year; or four digits), call verify_identity at once, even if the sentence trails off or the customer complains.\n- After each result, obey what_to_do_next: after a wrong answer ask the SAME question again; after a correct one ask the next question.\n- If the customer will not answer, or asks what the call is about instead of answering: explain once that the questions protect their account and that you can explain after the check. If they refuse or ask again, call transition_to_end_declined.\n- If they ask for a human: ask once whether you can help; if they insist, call transfer_to_agent with queue general.",
    "enter": {
     "llm": "The customer confirmed who they are. If their last message asked a question, answer it in one sentence first. Then say you need to ask two security questions, and ask the first one: their date of birth. Do not say why you are calling."
    },
    "tools": [
     "verify_identity",
     "transfer_to_agent"
    ],
    "tool_overrides": {
     "transfer_to_agent": {
      "description": "Hand the call to a person. CALL it only when the customer insists on a human after you asked once whether you can help. Queue general.",
      "fields": {
       "queue": {
        "enum": [
         "general"
        ]
       }
      }
     }
    },
    "turns": 20,
    "overflow": "end:declined",
    "verify": {
     "tool": "verify_identity",
     "questions": [
      {
       "field": "date_of_birth",
       "ask": "the date of birth",
       "kind": "date"
      },
      {
       "field": "phone_last4",
       "ask": "the last four digits of the phone number on file",
       "kind": "digits"
      }
     ],
     "args": {
      "field": "field",
      "answer": "answer"
     },
     "response": {
      "ok": "ok",
      "verified": "verified",
      "all_verified": "all_verified",
      "wrong_left": "wrong_answers_left",
      "hint": "hint",
      "swap_hint": "swap"
     },
     "on_pass": "fetch_account",
     "on_fail": "end:failed",
     "pass_description": "Both security questions passed"
    },
    "routes": [
     {
      "to": "end:transfer",
      "when": {
       "set": "transfer_queue"
      },
      "description": "The call was transferred"
     },
     {
      "to": "end:declined",
      "tool_call": true,
      "description": "The customer refused the security questions, or asked what the call is about instead of answering, a second time after you explained once why they are needed."
     }
    ]
   },
   "fetch_account": {
    "type": "fetch",
    "tool": "get_account",
    "retries": 2,
    "ok_field": "ok",
    "description": "Account loaded",
    "map": [
     {
      "path": "data.products",
      "var": "products"
     },
     {
      "path": "data.ok",
      "var": "account_ok"
     }
    ],
    "next": "compute_facts",
    "fail": "end:account_error"
   },
   "compute_facts": {
    "type": "compute",
    "next": "negotiate",
    "fail": "end:account_error"
   },
   "negotiate": {
    "type": "converse",
    "task": "The customer is verified. The debt has already been stated and you asked whether they can pay today.\n{{today_facts}}\n\n{{item_facts}}\n\nHOW TO GET A PROMISE\nA. AMOUNTS: you propose every amount. Never ask how much they can pay, even if they ask you to choose. Start with the full past-due amount. Only when the customer says the full amount is TOO MUCH (not for a plain no): offer that product's floor once, on the date of its next unused offer (offer 1's date if no offer was made yet), and say that the rest stays overdue. This floor offer takes the place of that offer, so a refusal moves on to the offer after it. From the moment the customer has said the full amount is too much, never propose the full amount for that product again: every later offer for it uses the floor. An amount the customer names from the floor up to the full amount is accepted. An amount below the floor cannot be recorded: say so and offer the floor once; if they do not accept it, that is a flat refusal.\nB. DATES: a promise date must be a business day, today or later, and not after that product's deadline. Look every date up in the CALENDAR below. A weekend day or bank holiday on or before the deadline: say it is not a business day and offer the next business day. A date after the deadline: say the latest date you can record is the deadline, and offer it; if they refuse it, that is a flat refusal. A vague period (\"next week\", \"end of the month\", \"when my salary comes\"): ask ONCE for the exact day; if they still give none, propose the deadline. Use DAY WORDS for \"tomorrow\", \"this Friday\" and \"next Friday\".\nC. A date or an amount the customer proposes is never a refusal: handle it with A and B.\nD. A flat refusal or a non-answer (a plain no, or a no to paying today with no date and no amount) moves that product to its NEXT offer in its OFFER LIST, said exactly as listed. A reply that does not answer your offer (small talk, a question back, or asking you to choose) is a non-answer: move to the next offer. Never say the same offer twice. When the list is used up, stop at once: the product is refused. Never add an offer that is not in the list and never repeat a refused offer.\nE. HARDSHIP: if the customer mentions illness, a death in the family or losing their job: say one short sentence of sympathy, make NO persuasion attempt, and offer ONCE to connect them with the customer support team. Never repeat this offer: if the reply does not clearly accept it, treat it as declined. If they accept, call transfer_to_agent with queue support. If they decline, that product ends as hardship with no further offer for it; continue with the next product and never offer support a second time. If they still name a date or an amount themselves, handle it as a promise.\nF. A request for a new payment plan, lower instalments or to restructure the debt: do not describe any plan; call transfer_to_agent with queue restructuring right away. A request for a discount or a waiver alone is not restructuring: say you cannot offer one and continue.\nG. A request for a human: ask once whether you can help; if they insist, call transfer_to_agent with queue general.\nH. \"I already paid\" for a product: say payments can take one business day to appear; that product ends as paid. \"This debt is not mine\", \"I never had this product\", or it was closed or paid off in the past: do not argue; that product ends as dispute. Then continue with the other products.\nI. If the customer says twice that they do not want to talk, every product without a promise ends as refused.\n\nORDER OF THE CONVERSATION\n1. If the customer can pay everything today: each product's promise is its full past-due amount, dated today.\n2. Otherwise go through the products one by one, in the order listed. Today was already asked for all products together: NEVER ask about paying today again. Your first offer for each product IS its offer 1, exactly as listed (amount and date), naming the product. If the customer already refused that product in an earlier answer, still make exactly that offer 1.\n3. ONE DATE FOR EVERYTHING: if the customer names one date for all products, accept it for all at once (do not ask product by product). Look the date up in the CALENDAR: every product it says TOO LATE for gets its own deadline instead, and the others keep the named date. Tell the customer this when you propose it.\nBefore any read-back, look up EVERY promise date in the CALENDAR line of that date and check that it says OK for that product. Never read back a date that the CALENDAR says is TOO LATE for that product.\n4. When every product has a promise or an end (refused, hardship, dispute, paid): if at least one product has a promise, say \"To confirm:\" and read ALL promises back together in one message - for each one the product name, the amount and the date, and say when a product got its own deadline instead of the named date - then ask \"Do you confirm?\". If the customer changes a date or an amount, check it with A and B and read back again. After a clear yes, call transition_to_record. If no product has a promise, call transition_to_record without a read-back.\nNever use the word \"recorded\" in this step: nothing is recorded yet. The only tools here are transfer_to_agent and transition_to_record.\n\n{{calendar_facts}}",
    "enter": {
     "tts": "{{summary_line}}"
    },
    "tools": [
     "transfer_to_agent"
    ],
    "tool_overrides": {
     "transfer_to_agent": {
      "description": "Hand the call to a person. CALL it with queue restructuring when the customer asks for a new payment plan or lower instalments; with queue support when a customer in hardship accepts the support team; with queue general when the customer insists on a human.",
      "fields": {
       "queue": {
        "enum": [
         "restructuring",
         "support",
         "general"
        ]
       }
      }
     }
    },
    "turns": 45,
    "overflow": "record",
    "routes": [
     {
      "to": "end:transfer",
      "when": {
       "set": "transfer_queue"
      },
      "description": "The call was transferred"
     },
     {
      "to": "record",
      "tool_call": true,
      "description": "The customer clearly confirmed the read-back of the promises (or, with several products, no product has a promise and every product has its end)."
     }
    ]
   },
   "record": {
    "type": "converse",
    "task": "Record what was agreed. Products:\n{{item_facts}}\n- For each product whose promise the customer confirmed in the read-back: call create_promise once, with its product_id, the amount and the date (YYYY-MM-DD) exactly as confirmed.\n- For each product without a promise: call add_note with its product_id and its reason: refused, hardship, dispute, paid, or service_error (when create_promise failed twice for it). In text, one short sentence why.\n- The customer has already confirmed: never ask \"Do you confirm?\" again in this step. If create_promise fails once, call it again at once with the same values, without asking the customer.\n- One tool call per message. After each tool result, obey what_to_do_next.\n- Never say a promise is recorded unless the tool result says it IS recorded. If a promise could not be recorded, tell the customer plainly.\n- When what_to_do_next says every product is handled: tell the customer in one or two sentences what was recorded and what could not be, then call transition_to_end_done.",
    "enter": {
     "llm": "The customer already confirmed the read-back. Your next message must be ONLY a tool call, with no words at all: call create_promise now for the first product with a confirmed promise, in product order, with exactly the amount and the date of the confirmed read-back. If no product has a promise, call add_note for the first product instead. Never ask for confirmation again, and never say \"recorded\" before a tool result says it IS recorded."
    },
    "tools": [
     "create_promise",
     "add_note"
    ],
    "turns": 15,
    "overflow": "end:done",
    "routes": [
     {
      "to": "end:done",
      "tool_call": true,
      "description": "Every product is handled: what_to_do_next said so and the customer was told what was recorded.",
      "done": true
     }
    ]
   }
  }
 },
